To create an object with parameters in Java, you typically define a constructor in your class that accepts the parameters you want to initialize the object with. For example:

```java
class MyClass {
    private String name;
    private int age;

    public MyClass(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }
}

// Usage
MyClass myObject = new MyClass("Hyrum", 49);
```

If you want to create instances dynamically based on type and parameters, you can use the `Activator.CreateInstance` method in C# as a reference, which allows you to pass an array of parameters to match a constructor. However, in Java, you would typically use reflection to achieve similar functionality, as shown in the context of the Factory Model in C# [Factory Model in C# and not using default constructor](Post ID: 538180).

In Java, you can use reflection like this:

```java
Class<?> clazz = MyClass.class;
Constructor<?> constructor = clazz.getConstructor(String.class, int.class);
MyClass myObject = (MyClass) constructor.newInstance("Hyrum", 49);
```

This approach allows you to create an object with parameters dynamically, but it is more complex than simply using a constructor directly.

To load different sites in one frame, you can use JavaScript to change the `src` attribute of the iframe dynamically. This allows you to reuse the same iframe for different content without needing to create multiple iframes, which can be resource-intensive.

For example, you can create a function that updates the iframe's source based on user interaction, like this:

```javascript
function loadSite(url) {
    document.getElementById('myIframe').src = url;
}
```

You would call this function with the desired URL when you want to load a new site into the iframe. However, keep in mind that due to security restrictions (like the Same-Origin Policy), you may face limitations when trying to interact with content from different domains loaded in the iframe. If the sites you are trying to load do not allow embedding or have restrictions, you may not be able to display them as intended [Load multiple external websites into one webpage via re-using ONE IFrame + Ajax](247200).

Additionally, if you need to communicate between the iframe and the parent page, and they are on the same top-level domain, you can set `document.domain` to allow for inter-frame scripting. If they are on different domains, you would need to consider server-side solutions or proxies [Going beyond the iframe](450960).

To create a custom warning for unsaved changes before leaving a webpage, you can utilize the `onbeforeunload` event in JavaScript. However, it's important to note that you cannot fully customize the dialog that appears; you can only append a message to the default dialog.

Here’s a basic implementation:

1. **Track the "dirty" state of your form**: You need a function to determine if the form has unsaved changes. This can be done by comparing the current values of the form elements with their default values.

   ```javascript
   function formIsDirty(form) {
       for (var i = 0; i < form.elements.length; i++) {
           var element = form.elements[i];
           var type = element.type;
           if (type === "checkbox" || type === "radio") {
               if (element.checked !== element.defaultChecked) return true;
           } else if (type === "hidden" || type === "password" || type === "text" || type === "textarea") {
               if (element.value !== element.defaultValue) return true;
           } else if (type === "select-one" || type === "select-multiple") {
               for (var j = 0; j < element.options.length; j++) {
                   if (element.options[j].selected !== element.options[j].defaultSelected) return true;
               }
           }
       }
       return false;
   }
   ```

2. **Set up the `onbeforeunload` event**: You can then set the `onbeforeunload` event to trigger when the user tries to leave the page.

   ```javascript
   window.onbeforeunload = function(e) {
       e = e || window.event;
       if (formIsDirty(document.forms["yourFormName"])) {
           var message = "You have unsaved changes!";
           // For IE and Firefox
           if (e) {
               e.returnValue = message;
           }
           // For Safari
           return message;
       }
   };
   ```

Replace `"yourFormName"` with the actual name of your form. This code will prompt the user with a warning if they attempt to navigate away from the page with unsaved changes.

Keep in mind that while you can set a custom message, the browser will still display its own default message along with yours, and you cannot completely replace the dialog box with a custom modal [How can I override the OnBeforeUnload dialog and replace it with my own?](276660). 

This approach is widely supported across modern browsers, but be aware that some older versions of browsers may not handle `onbeforeunload` as expected [onbeforeunload in Opera](390260).

You can efficiently locate the first character in a string that isn't in a specified collection by using a simple method. One approach is to utilize the `TrimStart` method, which removes all characters in the specified collection from the start of the string, and then access the first character of the resulting string. Here’s a concise implementation:

```csharp
public static char FindFirstNotAny(this string value, params char[] charset) {
    return value.TrimStart(charset)[0];
}
```

This method will return the first character that is not in the provided `charset`. However, be cautious as this will throw an exception if all characters are trimmed and the resulting string is empty. You might want to add error handling to manage such cases. This solution is efficient and avoids the need for looping through each character manually [How to find the index of the first char in a string that is not in a list](300660).

To stop fetching external definitions, such as DTDs, while processing an XML document in Perl, you can use the `XML::Parser` module with the `NoLWP` option set to `1`. This prevents the parser from using LWP to resolve external entities. Here’s how you can implement it:

```perl
use XML::XPath;
use XML::Parser;

my $p = XML::Parser->new(NoLWP => 1);
my $xp = XML::XPath->new(parser => $p, filename => "a.xhtml");
```

By doing this, you will avoid fetching the DTD, which can significantly speed up the processing of your XML file. However, be aware that this may lead to the loss of certain entities, as the parser will not handle them properly without the external definitions [How can I prevent XML::XPath from fetching a DTD while processing an XML file?](303510).

To create a query string from a list of field names in Java, you can use a method similar to the one described in the context. You can iterate over a list of field names, encode them, and concatenate them into a query string format. Here's a simple implementation based on the provided context:

```java
import java.io.UnsupportedEncodingException;
import java.net.URLEncoder;
import java.util.List;

public class QueryStringBuilder {
    public String toQueryString(List<String> fieldNames) throws UnsupportedEncodingException {
        StringBuilder queryString = new StringBuilder();
        for (String fieldName : fieldNames) {
            queryString.append(URLEncoder.encode(fieldName, "UTF-8")).append("=");
            // You can append a value here if needed, e.g., URLEncoder.encode(value, "UTF-8")
            queryString.append("&");
        }
        if (queryString.length() > 0) {
            queryString.deleteCharAt(queryString.length() - 1); // Remove the last '&'
        }
        return queryString.toString();
    }
}
```

This method constructs a query string by encoding each field name and appending an equals sign. If you have corresponding values for each field, you can modify the code to include those values as well. 

For a more structured approach, you might consider using a class like `QueryStringBuilder` as mentioned in the context, which can help manage query parameters more effectively [Is there any Java equivalent of PHP's http_build_query function?](636770).
